## 5️⃣ Minimal “drop‑in” change (if you want just one line)

If you are not ready to overhaul the whole startup flow, you can keep `dotenv` for local dev **and** add a guard that refuses to start unless a specific “required” secret is defined. This is a tiny safety net:

```js
// At the top of app.js, after dotenv
require('dotenv').config();

const requiredSecrets = ['JWT_SECRET', 'DATABASE_URL'];
const missing = requiredSecrets.filter(k => !process.env[k]);

if (missing.length) {
  console.error(`✖️  Missing required environment variables: ${missing.join(', ')}`);
  process.exit(1); // fail fast
}
```

This will still allow you to later switch the source of those variables without changing any application code.

