### How each change mitigates the findings

| Change | OWASP issue addressed | Why it helps |
|--------|----------------------|--------------|
| **`helmet()`** | A05 – Security Misconfiguration | Sets secure HTTP headers (CSP, HSTS, X‑Frame‑Options, etc.) that protect against XSS, click‑jacking, MIME sniffing. |
| **CORS whitelist** | A01 – Broken Access Control (reduces cross‑origin data leakage) | Prevents browsers from letting malicious origins read data from your API. |
| **`express-rate-limit`** | A04 – Insecure Design / A10 – Potential DoS | Throttles abusive IPs, lowering brute‑force and denial‑of‑service risk. |
| **JWT auth (`authenticateJwt`)** | A01 – Broken Access Control, A07 – Authentication | Only clients possessing a signed token can reach protected routes (e.g., `/api/users`). |
| **Input validation (`express-validator`)** | A03 – Injection & A01 – Broken Access Control | Guarantees that any data entering the system conforms to expected format; prevents SQL/NoSQL injection when DB queries are added later. |
| **Central error handler** | A05 – Misconfiguration | Hides stack traces from callers, avoiding information disclosure. |
| **`morgan` logging** | A09 – Logging & Monitoring | Provides a request audit trail for detection and forensics. |
| **Environment variables (`dotenv`)** | A02 – Cryptographic Failures | Keeps secrets (e.g., JWT signing key) out of source code and version control. |
| **Prefix API routes with `/api/`** | General | Gives you a clean separation for future versioning and makes it easy to apply group‑wide security policies (rate‑limit, auth). |

