| Step | Action | Reason |
|------|--------|--------|
| **1️⃣ Remove `.env.test` from source control** | `git rm --cached .env.test && echo ".env.test" >> .gitignore` | Prevent accidental commit of any future real secrets. |
| **2️⃣ Store the canary values in a secret manager** (AWS, Vault, GCP – whichever you already use). | e.g., create an AWS secret named `my-app-test` containing the JSON from `.env.test`. | Keeps the data out of the repo and allows rotation without redeploy. |
| **3️⃣ Add a tiny bootstrap module** (`config.js`) that fetches those secrets at startup (see the AWS example above). | Centralises secret loading; you can swap the underlying provider without touching the rest of the code. |
| **4️⃣ Refactor any code that would have used `process.env.*`** to read from the loaded `secrets` object instead. | Guarantees you aren’t accidentally falling back to undefined env vars. |
| **5️⃣ Harden the deployment pipeline** – inject only the minimal **runtime token** (e.g., an IAM role or Vault token) needed to fetch the secret. | The container/pod never receives the raw secret values at build time. |
| **6️⃣ Add a health‑check that verifies secret availability** (e.g., try a DB ping using `secrets.DATABASE_URL` and log a failure). | Detects mis‑configuration early. |
| **7️⃣ Run secret‑access tests in CI** – make sure the code fails clearly if the secret cannot be retrieved (so you notice missing permissions). | Prevents “works locally but not in prod” surprises. |

