## 5️⃣ Checklist for “Secrets in Code” Hygiene  

| ✅ | Action |
|---|--------|
| **✅** | Add `*.env*` (and any other secret‑containing filenames) to `.gitignore`. |
| **✅** | Keep a **template** `.env.example` that only lists variable names, not values. |
| **✅** | Switch to a **managed secret store** (AWS Secrets Manager, Vault, etc.) for production. |
| **✅** | Use a **wrapper module** (`config/secrets.js`) that validates existence and caches values. |
| **✅** | Fail fast on missing secrets (`process.exit(1)`). |
| **✅** | Rotate secrets regularly (AWS Secrets Manager can auto‑rotate DB passwords, API keys). |
| **✅** | Restrict IAM policies for the service role / EC2 instance that reads the secret (least‑privilege). |
| **✅** | Remove any **hard‑coded** canary values from source history (git filter‑repo or BFG). |
| **✅** | Enable **audit logging** on your secrets manager so you can detect unexpected accesses. |
| **✅** | Document the required environment variables in your README and CI pipeline. |

